# roar state — the generated setup stage runs `roar init`, which creates
# .roar/. Without this rule the worktree goes dirty and the FIRST PAID
# `roar run` refuses to execute ("Run blocked: untracked files").
.roar/
# --- reproduction outputs (scaffold_row.py: mnist rule) ---
# file-pattern ignore + tracked .gitkeep so each dir survives a clean
# checkout and roar keeps the outputs in lineage. NEVER dir-ignore.
#
# Each output dir is re-included one path component at a time before its
# contents are ignored. git cannot re-include a file whose PARENT DIRECTORY
# is excluded — it never descends — so a bare `!<dir>/.gitkeep` is dead text
# under an upstream `<dir>/` rule, and every .gitkeep silently goes
# unreachable while this tool reports success. Row 022 hit exactly that:
# upstream ignored `datasets/` and `checkpoints/`, so all 7 .gitkeep files
# were unreachable and roar would have dropped the outputs. Re-including
# each ancestor and immediately re-ignoring its contents keeps sibling
# paths ignored, so this stays safe when nothing shadows the dir.
!data/
data/*
!outputs/
outputs/*
!outputs/3B/
outputs/3B/*
!data/.gitkeep
!outputs/.gitkeep
!outputs/3B/.gitkeep
!artifacts/
artifacts/*
!artifacts/.gitkeep
